<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">River Irfon</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The River Irfon is a river in mid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> that rises in the Cambrian Mountains and flows south-east through Llanwrtyd <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">Wells</a> to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-wye">River Wye</a> at Builth Wells. Its valley, with the middle Wye, formed the early medieval Welsh kingdom of Buellt, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llywelyn-ap-gruffudd">Llywelyn ap Gruffudd</a> was killed near its banks at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilmeri">Cilmeri</a> in 1282.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/river-irfon.jpg" alt="River Irfon" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Roger Kidd · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Afon_Irfon,_below_the_Devil%27s_Staircase,_Powys_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1071151.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
